WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.587
[Music]

00:00:01.587 --> 00:00:03.866
I lead the CHARM lab. It's the Care Health And

00:00:03.866 --> 00:00:09.720
Reasoning Machines Lab. And we develop&nbsp;
machine learning tools to better utilize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:15.967
electronic health records data for patient&nbsp;
characterization and for risk assessment.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.507
Part of my work focuses on longitudinal data&nbsp;
that comes from electronic health records so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.507 --> 00:00:27.300
that we can do forecasting, so that we&nbsp;
can know a patient's risk for particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.760
sequelae of disease forward in time, or&nbsp;
we can at least estimate it or predict it.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:41.194
Another part of my work focuses on Equitable&nbsp;
Risk Assessments so that the models that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.194 --> 00:00:46.598
develop have performance characteristics&nbsp;
across different subgroups that may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.598 --> 00:00:52.291
of interest. We don't want the models to&nbsp;
underperform in any particular subgroup.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.528
A third line of work is to better&nbsp;
understand electronic health records&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.528 --> 00:01:01.920
data because electronic health records&nbsp;
data are a little bit different than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.041
other types of clinical data in&nbsp;
that they're passively collected.

00:01:06.041 --> 00:01:09.891
They're collected by indication as&nbsp;
in the provider has to seek out that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:13.071
information for it to be entered into&nbsp;
the electronic health record. Whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.071 --> 00:01:16.726
or not something is there is&nbsp;
makes the data incomplete.

00:01:16.726 --> 00:01:19.320
Not every patient is going to&nbsp;
have every measurement collected.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:23.440
And so, we try to understand how do we&nbsp;
build models that capture this type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:29.219
data irregularity and be able to use it for&nbsp;
prediction and for characterization purposes.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.340
I think that better healthcare&nbsp;
going forward is data-based policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:43.273
data-based healthcare. And that's why we need to&nbsp;
develop tools and methods that help utilize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.273 --> 00:01:47.655
data the best we can so that we can make&nbsp;
the most informed decisions for patients.

00:01:48.094 --> 00:01:51.257
[Music]

00:01:51.257 --> 00:01:54.476
[Music fades to silence]

